Inventory|Ansible inventory defines managed hosts, groups and associated connection or variable data.|Static files and dynamic plugins provide targets. Group membership influences variable resolution and play selection. Inspect the resolved inventory before a fleet-wide action so an unexpected group cannot broaden the target set.|ansible-inventory -i inventory.yml --graph
Playbooks|A playbook describes plays that apply ordered tasks to selected hosts.|Each play establishes hosts, variables and execution settings; tasks invoke modules. Execution can vary with conditions, handlers and failure strategy. Read the target list and task order before assuming a playbook affects only one service.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --list-tasks
Modules|Modules implement individual operations such as managing packages, files or services.|Purpose-built modules often understand desired state and report meaningful changes better than shell commands. Their check-mode and diff support varies. Use module documentation to understand required parameters and side effects.|ansible-doc ansible.builtin.copy
Idempotence|Idempotence means repeating automation with the same desired state should not produce additional unintended changes.|Modules compare actual state with requested state where possible. A shell command that always appends a line is not idempotent. Test a second run and inspect changed results rather than treating a successful first run as sufficient.|
Become|Privilege escalation lets Ansible perform selected tasks as another user through mechanisms such as sudo.|The SSH connection user and become user are distinct. Apply escalation only where needed and verify the target policy. Broad root execution can hide ownership mistakes and increase the impact of incorrect inventory.|
Variables|Variables parameterize tasks, templates and roles.|Values can come from inventory, playbooks, roles and runtime inputs with defined precedence. Unexpected overrides often explain environment-specific behavior. Keep sources clear and avoid relying on obscure precedence to encode critical production differences.|
Facts|Facts describe information gathered from managed hosts, such as operating system and network details.|Fact gathering invokes setup or other collectors and can add overhead. Cached facts may become stale. Use facts for supported platform branching, but verify assumptions before destructive storage or network changes.|ansible localhost -m ansible.builtin.setup -a 'filter=ansible_distribution'
Handlers|Handlers run actions notified by tasks that report changes.|They usually run at defined flush points and coalesce repeated notifications. This makes a single restart possible after several configuration updates. Failure behavior and force-handlers settings affect whether a notified handler eventually runs.|
Check mode|Check mode asks supported modules to predict changes without applying them.|Not every module supports prediction, and tasks depending on registered results can behave differently. It is a planning aid, not proof that a real run is harmless. Validate in an isolated representative environment as well.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --check
Diff mode|Diff mode displays supported before-and-after changes, especially for text files.|It makes configuration review easier but can expose secrets in templates or file contents. Combine it with check mode where supported and disable diff for sensitive tasks. A diff shows proposed bytes, not application validity.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --check --diff
Vault|Ansible Vault encrypts selected files or variable values at rest.|Authorized execution decrypts values for use, so logs and generated files still need protection. Separate vault credentials from encrypted data and scope access by environment. Encryption does not replace least privilege on target systems.|ansible-vault view secrets.yml
Templates|Templates render configuration files using variables and Jinja expressions.|The template module compares rendered output with the destination and can notify handlers on change. Validate generated syntax before replacing a working configuration. Avoid embedding environment-specific secrets in broadly readable artifacts.|
Roles|Roles organize reusable tasks, handlers, defaults, variables and files.|They provide a conventional structure and an interface through variables. Keep dependencies and assumptions documented so the role can be tested independently. A role should not silently change unrelated system settings.|
Collections|Collections package modules, plugins, roles and documentation under a namespace.|Versioned collection requirements make controller environments reproducible. Pin and review updates because module behavior can change. The controller's installed collection version is part of the automation's effective implementation.|ansible-galaxy collection list
FQCNs|A fully qualified collection name identifies a plugin or module by namespace and collection.|For example, ansible.builtin.copy explicitly selects the built-in copy module. Qualification avoids ambiguity when collections provide similarly named plugins and makes documentation lookup clearer.|ansible-doc ansible.builtin.service
Conditionals|Conditionals decide whether a task runs for a host based on evaluated data.|when expressions can use facts, variables and registered results. Handle undefined values deliberately and avoid assuming string values behave like booleans. A skipped prerequisite may leave later tasks without the data they expect.|
Registered results|A registered result stores a task's returned fields for later decisions.|Return structures differ by module; command results often include rc, stdout and stderr. Inspect documented fields before writing conditions. A skipped task or failed command can produce a different result shape.|
Loops|Loops apply a task to a sequence of items.|Each item produces its own result, and registered loop output contains a results collection. Label loops to keep logs readable and avoid leaking secrets through item values. Repeated service restarts inside loops can often be replaced with a handler.|
Failure conditions|failed_when defines which result should count as task failure.|Some commands return nonzero statuses for expected conditions, while a zero status may still carry an invalid result. Express the application's actual failure rule. Broad ignore_errors can allow broken prerequisites to cascade into misleading later success.|
Change reporting|changed_when defines whether a task reports a state change.|Accurate reporting drives handlers and helps detect drift. Marking every shell task unchanged hides real changes; marking every read-only task changed causes unnecessary restarts. Prefer state-aware modules and define custom conditions where necessary.|
Serial rollout|serial limits the number or percentage of hosts processed in each play batch.|Batching preserves capacity during maintenance and lets health gates stop expansion. Verify group size and failure thresholds before use. A batch size of one still needs meaningful validation between hosts.|
Delegation|Delegation runs a task on a different host from the play's current target.|It supports load-balancer operations or controller-side API calls while iterating application hosts. Variable and connection context need attention, and delegated tasks may still execute concurrently. Protect shared delegated resources from races.|
Run once|run_once schedules a task once for the applicable execution batch rather than for every host.|With serial execution, it can run once per batch. Host-dependent conditions and variables may come from the selected host. Use a dedicated play or explicit coordination when an operation must occur exactly once across an entire deployment.|
Blocks and rescue|Blocks group tasks and attach shared settings, rescue handling and cleanup behavior.|A rescue section can handle qualifying task failures; always can perform cleanup. Unreachable hosts and some invalid task definitions do not behave like ordinary task failures. Recovery must still verify whether partially applied changes remain.|
Async tasks|Async execution lets long-running tasks continue beyond the normal synchronous task duration.|poll controls waiting behavior and async sets a time bound. With poll zero, follow up using async_status rather than assuming completion. Avoid launching unbounded concurrent work against a shared dependency.|
Tags|Tags select subsets of tasks for execution.|They speed focused operations but may skip prerequisites or validation. Document supported tag combinations and test them. A tagged task can still depend on variables or facts normally prepared by omitted tasks.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --list-tags
Dynamic inventory|Dynamic inventory discovers hosts from external systems through plugins.|The resulting membership can change between runs, so verify the target snapshot and filtering rules. Credentials, caching and naming conventions affect correctness. A discovery API failure must not silently become an unintended empty or broad deployment.|
Concurrency|Ansible concurrency determines how many hosts and tasks can progress simultaneously.|forks, serial, throttle and strategy settings control different dimensions. More concurrency can overwhelm package mirrors, APIs or shared databases. Choose limits from dependency capacity and deployment failure domains.|
Secret logging|Automation can expose sensitive values in task arguments, diffs, registered results or debug output.|no_log suppresses selected task output but does not secure every downstream use or generated file. Keep secrets out of loop labels and broad debug dumps. Protect controller logs and target-file permissions.|
Automation validation|Validation checks syntax, targets, rendered configuration and runtime behavior before broad execution.|Combine syntax checks, inventory review, supported check mode and a representative test host. Run twice to assess idempotence and verify application health after changes. A syntax-valid playbook can still target the wrong machines.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --syntax-check
